findings.body:
The three call sites in `prepare_tools` use `write_and_make_executable(...)?`, which returns `Result<(), FileWriteError>`, inside a function returning `crate::Result`. That requires `From<FileWriteError> for crate::Error`. The `Error` enum in `crates/tauri-bundler/src/error.rs` has `#[from]` only for `io::Error`, `anyhow::Error`, and other concrete types, so the conversion does not exist and the `?` will not compile.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="229-241"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The simplest fix is to derive `thiserror::Error` on `FileWriteError` and add a `#[from]`-style variant on `crate::Error`, or to have `write_and_make_executable` return `crate::Result` and map the errors with `Context`. The existing `Error::Fs { context, path, error }` variant is the natural place for path-aware permission and write errors.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="309-313"}

The PR swaps the order in `write_and_make_executable` so `fs::set_permissions` runs before `fs::write`. `set_permissions` follows the path, so it fails with ENOENT when the file has not been created yet. Every first-run call, meaning every `!exists()` branch in `prepare_tools` on a fresh tools directory, will now return `FileWriteError::Permissions` and abort the bundle. The original write-then-chmod order was correct.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="309-316"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The error variant is also misleading here. The failure is a missing file, not a permission problem, so the message would send a user looking in the wrong place.

The GTK branch was changed from `if !gtk.exists()` to `if gtk.exists()`. On a clean tools directory the file does not exist, so the embedded script is never written. linuxdeploy then runs without the GTK plugin, and the AppImage is built without GTK resources, schemas, and modules. On later runs the file exists, so the bundled copy is overwritten on every build. The result is that the first build and later builds produce different AppImages.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="242-247"}

The GStreamer branch keeps `!gstreamer.exists()`, so the two branches are now inconsistent.

The original code mapped `i686` to `i383` and used that name for both the local filename and the download URL. The PR keeps the local name on `linuxdeploy_arch` but builds the URL from `arch`. For `i686`, the fetch now requests `linuxdeploy-i686.AppImage` where it previously requested `linuxdeploy-i383.AppImage`. The PR description does not mention this, and nothing in the diff shows that the release asset was renamed. If the asset is still named `i383`, 32-bit builds will fail to download linuxdeploy.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="235-240"}

I haven't checked the binary-releases asset list, so this is a question for the author rather than a confirmed break.

The two scripts are snapshots of upstream `master` with no version, commit hash, or checksum recorded in the repo. Upstream fixes will no longer reach users unless someone manually re-vendors the files. Because the GStreamer branch still checks `!exists()`, any user who already has a cached `linuxdeploy-plugin-gstreamer.sh` from the old download will keep that older copy and never get the embedded version. The GTK branch overwrites every time, so the two plugins now behave differently across runs.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="248-252"}

The trade-off is worth stating in the PR. Embedding removes the network dependency, but it also removes automatic updates.
